TILE-KEYED BORROWS — feasibility and prices for the three variants (issue #455)
================================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P + 4 E cores (12 logical), Darwin 25.5.0.
Base      main ee07b00 (766/766; 1.283 / 1.583 / 1.623 / 1.903 at 8bpc
          t=1/2/4/8 per the #469 verification round).
Branch    perf/tile-keyed.
Build     cargo build --release, NO -C target-cpu=native. Every behavioural
          change is behind a probe-* / __probe_* feature; `default` carries
          only the inert TileKey field and the recon key stores.
Timing    NO `nice` on any timed run. Two-point wall fit (2 and 20 frames,
          beta = (hi - lo) / 18) so process startup drops out.
Design    docs/TILE_KEYED_BORROWS.md — the feasibility half, with file:line.

RESULT, up front
  Variant 2 (shard by tile index) is a MEASURED NEGATIVE on its own:
  tkshard/base 1.0285 / 1.0338 / 0.9964 / 0.9712 at 8bpc t=1/2/4/8 and
  1.0722 / 1.0687 / 1.0490 / 1.0228 at 10bpc. With 128 shards the existing
  multiplicative address hash already separates concurrent tile columns; the
  key's own store and compare cost 2-7% where there is nothing to win.

  Variant 3 (the strided hull, which the key makes legal) is the win, and only
  at 8bpc: tkboth/base 1.0203 / 0.8868 / 0.8490 / 0.8287 at t=1/2/4/8, taking
  base/dav1d 1.303 / 1.580 / 1.631 / 1.860 to 1.329 / 1.401 / 1.385 / 1.541.
  10bpc moves 1.0785 / 0.9939 / 0.9693 / 0.9285.

  It does NOT beat #469, whose verification round reports 1.309 / 1.332 /
  1.323 / 1.477 at the same 8bpc cells (quoted from its record, not re-measured
  here). Tile identity is a cleaner key than an address-derived column band --
  exact, no division, no per-alloc column stores -- and on this vector a slower
  one.

  n = 4 complete rounds at 8bpc, n = 3 at 10bpc -- 168 rows, 21 cells
  discarded, sum of the foreign-load column = 0. BELOW this campaign's n >= 7
  bar, and the reason is coordination rather than instrumentation: another
  agent's test suite owned the box for most of the window, and when it cleared,
  that agent started its OWN timed sweep (~/tmp/indver/bin/bench_*). At that
  point both sweeps were polling for an idle box and each was making the
  other's cells dirty. I STOPPED MINE rather than keep discarding, because
  every discarded attempt is ~90 s of decoding loaded onto their measurement.
  Same conclusion the site-class round reached: two timed campaigns cannot
  share this box. The remaining rounds were not run; they were given up.

Files
  tile_keyed_gap_2026-08-09.tsv        gap sweep, one row per (round, arm,
                                       vector, threads); last column is the
                                       foreign-load count for that cell.
  tile_keyed_census_2026-08-09.tsv     registrations/frame by disposition
                                       (keyed / unkeyed-sharded / single-shard).
  tile_keyed_stitch_2026-08-09.tsv     Variant 1's copy cost and the
                                       recon-write-locality control.
  tile_keyed_keychannel_2026-08-09.tsv per-call cost of the three ways the tile
                                       key could reach the tracker.
  tile_keyed_bands_2026-08-09.txt      per-arm min/median/max with a
                                       disjoint/OVERLAP verdict per cell, for
                                       tkboth-vs-base, tkshard-vs-base and
                                       base-vs-main.

Arms
  main      pristine ee07b00. Isolates the cost of the WithOffset key FIELD and
            the recon key stores, which are the only things `default` gains.
  base      this branch, default features. Key present, tracker unchanged.
  tkshard   + tile-keyed shard selection. Per-row REFERENCES unchanged, so no
            aliasing question; only the tracker's bookkeeping is relaxed.
  tkboth    + the strided hull for keyed references. CEILING ONLY: two tile
            columns' hull guards are two live overlapping &mut, which is UB by
            Rust's aliasing model whatever the tracker records (the PR #470
            lesson from the other direction). Its wall number is an upper
            bound from a build with UB in it, not a candidate.
  untr      tracker compiled out (probe-untracked) — the ceiling this campaign
            already carries.
  dav1d_fd1 dav1d 1.5.4 --framedelay 1, the tile-threading-only model we
            implement.

  tkhull (the hull WITHOUT the key) is deliberately absent from the timed grid
  because it DOES NOT DECODE at t > 1 on either depth:
      overlapping DisjointMut:
       current: &mut _[960..58576] at include/dav1d/picture.rs:804
      existing: &mut _[0..57616]
  Two 16x16 block hulls at pixel columns 0 and 960 — the tile-column boundary
  at 3840/4. They share no byte; their hulls overlap because a hull spans the
  inter-row gaps, which belong to the next tile column. That is #467's
  documented false positive, reproduced, and it is exactly what the key removes.

Correctness
  md5_inventory                766 PASS / 0 mismatch / 0 error / 2 skip.
  set-diff BY NAME, md5 as the value, against
  benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst:
                               only in baseline 0 / only in head 0 /
                               DIFFERING 0   (768 rows both sides)
  cross-arm frame md5          8bpc  a00c11f454328023c58af14d55544cff
                               10bpc 4f218411bc6ee4cc9c630fe827337fa2
                               identical over {main, base, tkshard, tkboth,
                               untr} x t = 1, 2, 4, 8 x both depths (40 cells;
                               tkhull matches at t=1 and fails to decode above).
  cargo test --release         157 passed, 0 failed, 28 ignored — including
                               decode_md5_verify 14/14, decode_permutations
                               19/19, mt_stress, tile_threading_overlap.
  multi_decoder_pressure       12 concurrent decoders x 3 iters x t =
                               1/2/4/8/16 over v4k_8tile, v4k_8tile_10b, v1024,
                               v256: PASS on the DEFAULT build AND on tkboth,
                               every md5 matching the serial reference. Worth
                               stating for tkboth specifically: the hull's
                               theoretical false-positive risk (an unkeyed
                               borrow whose address hash lands on a tile's
                               shard) did not fire under oversubscription.

Soundness gates
  tests/tile_key.rs            6 mechanism tests, green WITH and WITHOUT
                               __probe_tilekey_shard (the file asserts the
                               inert behaviour when the feature is off, so it
                               cannot go vacuous by being built wrong — which
                               is how the hull cfg bug below was caught).
  teeth, 3 mutations           key as a global off-switch      -> 3/6 FAIL
                               keyed twins dropping the key    -> 3/6 FAIL
                               shard index ignoring the key    -> 3/6 FAIL
                               each restored by sha256 AND git diff --exit-code.
  mandatory hazard A           add's in-lock `state` re-read deleted (4af62ae)
                               -> wide_exclusion FAILS under --features
                               __probe_wide. Restored byte-exact, green again.
  mandatory hazard B           active() cut to one shard
                               -> wide_exclusion FAILS. Restored byte-exact.
  forbid(unsafe_code)          proved ACTIVELY: a planted unsafe block gives
                               "error: usage of an `unsafe` block" against
                               #![forbid(unsafe_code)] at src/lib.rs:1.

A bug the liveness counter caught that no test would have
  The first hull build named the SUB-CRATE's feature in a cfg inside the parent
  crate (`#[cfg(feature = "__probe_tilekey_hull")]` in include/dav1d/picture.rs,
  where the feature is spelled `probe-tilekey-hull`). It compiled, decoded
  correctly, and measured identically to base — a perfect silent null. The
  registration COUNT is what exposed it: 26,484,179/frame on both arms, to the
  byte. Assert on the mechanism, not the outcome.

Instrument note
  probe-tilekey-count puts three fetch_adds on the hottest path in the decoder
  and the build runs ~20x slow (1,323 ms/frame at t=8 against a real 65). Its
  COUNTS are load-bearing; its ms are not, and no timed arm carries it. Its
  totals also do not reconcile with the campaign's probe-sites figure of
  22,700,725/frame at t=8 (this instrument reports 26,484,179 for the same
  cell). Two different instruments, not reconciled here — the arm-to-arm
  DELTAS, which is what liveness needs, come from one instrument throughout.

The carrier is not free at 10bpc
  base (this branch, default features) against pristine main:
    8bpc  t=1/2/4/8   1.0070 / 0.9992 / 1.0042 / 0.9901   all OVERLAP
    10bpc t=1         1.0084                              OVERLAP
    10bpc t=2         1.0164                              DISJOINT
    10bpc t=4         1.0207                              DISJOINT
    10bpc t=8         1.0084                              OVERLAP
  Nothing measurable at 8bpc; 1.6-2.1% at 10bpc t=2/t=4, where two bytes per
  pixel make WithOffset 24 bytes instead of 16 in a Copy struct passed by value
  through every kernel. A real cost of the CHANNEL before any tracker change,
  and part of why 10bpc barely moves.
